  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# ZeRO Optimizer State Sharding Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` implements:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- `flatten_params(module)` and `unflatten_into(module, flat)` that pack a model's parameters into one contiguous tensor and unpack back. The flat layout is what makes sharding by rank a simple slice."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- `ZeroOptimizer(model, world_size, rank, lr)` that owns the rank's shard of the master copy and Adam moments."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- `step()` that runs reduce_scatter on the flat gradient, applies Adam to the rank's shard, and allgathers the updated parameters back."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- A demo that trains a 3-layer MLP for 20 steps and prints the per-step memory budget alongside a vanilla DDP baseline."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Run it:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```bash"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "python3 code/main.py"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Output: per-step loss and the memory table that shows ZeRO-1 holds 1/N of the optimiser state on each rank versus DDP's full copy."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Extend to ZeRO-2 by sharding gradients: each rank only stores the gradient for its shard, achieved by zeroing out the non-shard portion after backward.\n2. Add a memory profiler that prints actual fp32 byte usage on rank 0 versus the formula prediction.\n3. Measure the per-step wall-clock time of vanilla DDP versus ZeRO-1 and decompose into forward, backward, comm.\n4. Implement gradient clipping under ZeRO-1: the L2 norm must be computed across all shards via allreduce of the local norm squared.\n5. Implement a \"naive ZeRO\" with allreduce instead of reduce_scatter, measure the wire-time difference. Defend the reduce_scatter choice with numbers."
      ]
    }
  ],
